  "query_preview": "..Very strong high pressure continues to dominate the \nweather across Arizona, with a gradual shift to the north over the \nnext few days. Dry air aloft continues to inhibit convective \ndevelopment. Additionally, the dust lofted into the air along with \ndebris clouds have remained across much of central Arizona today. As \na result, expect isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nacross the higher terrain, mainly focused over eastern Arizona \nthrough Tuesday. Storm activity remains diurnally driven with \nshowers and storms dissipating as we head into the evening. \n\nHeat remains the main story through much the week, especially over \nthe Grand Canyon where the heat will linger through the weekend. \nExtreme Heat Warnings continue over the Grand Canyon and Glen and \nMarble Canyons through Wednesday but will likely need to be \nextended, possibly into the weekend. \n\nAs the high starts to push to the north, an increase in moisture \noccurs and temperatures will slightly cool. The placement of the \nhigh looks to remain in a favorable position for the region to allow \nfor the monsoon to ramp up for a couple of days as we head towards \nthe end of the week. Expect daily chances for isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms each day, especially across the higher \nterrain. A slight downturn in activity may occur over the weekend \nbut does not have a lot of support in the operational models at this \ntime. With the high not moving much, continue to expect a more \nactive pattern to return with continued hot conditions.",
